(go/type stkframe "A stkframe holds information about a single physical stack frame.\n" (struct


    ^{:line 18 :tag funcInfo :doc "fn is the function being run in this frame. If there is\ninlining, this is the outermost function.\n"} fn

























    ^{:line 44 :tag uintptr :doc "pc is the program counter within fn.\n\nThe meaning of this is subtle:\n\n- Typically, this frame performed a regular function call\n  and this is the return PC (just after the CALL\n  instruction). In this case, pc-1 reflects the CALL\n  instruction itself and is the correct source of symbolic\n  information.\n\n- If this frame \"called\" sigpanic, then pc is the\n  instruction that panicked, and pc is the correct address\n  to use for symbolic information.\n\n- If this is the innermost frame, then PC is where\n  execution will continue, but it may not be the\n  instruction following a CALL. This may be from\n  cooperative preemption, in which case this is the\n  instruction after the call to morestack. Or this may be\n  from a signal or an un-started goroutine, in which case\n  PC could be any instruction, including the first\n  instruction in a function. Conventionally, we use pc-1\n  for symbolic information, unless pc == fn.entry(), in\n  which case we use pc.\n"} pc









    ^{:line 54 :tag uintptr :doc "continpc is the PC where execution will continue in fn, or\n0 if execution will not continue in this frame.\n\nThis is usually the same as pc, unless this frame \"called\"\nsigpanic, in which case it's either the address of\ndeferreturn or 0 if this frame will never execute again.\n\nThis is the PC to use to look up GC liveness for this frame.\n"} continpc

    ^{:line 56 :tag uintptr} lr ; program counter at caller aka link register
    ^{:line 57 :tag uintptr} sp ; stack pointer at pc
    ^{:line 58 :tag uintptr} fp ; stack pointer at caller aka frame pointer
    ^{:line 59 :tag uintptr} varp ; top of local variables
    ^{:line 60 :tag uintptr} argp)) ; pointer to function arguments




